<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dahshur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dahshur is a royal necropolis on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The Fourth Dynasty king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu</a> built two pyramids there in the 26th century BCE, the Bent Pyramid and the Red Pyramid, the first true smooth-sided pyramid. Kings of the Middle Kingdom also built pyramids at Dahshur, and the burials of their royal women yielded fine jewellery.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dahshur.jpg" alt="Dahshur" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Mayer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dahshur_-_Red_Pyramid_-_Tourist_policemen_on_camel.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dahshur is an ancient Egyptian pyramid complex and necropolis on the Western Desert plateau, at the edge of the cultivated plain. Together with the pyramid complexes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a>, Abusir and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a> to its north it forms the pyramid fields of the ancient capital city of Memphis. It is listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> and shares its name with the nearby village of Manshiyyat Dahshur.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The pyramids of Dahshur gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> the experience needed to move from step-sided to smooth-sided pyramids, and so to build the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-pyramid-of-giza">Great Pyramid of Giza</a>. The first was the Bent Pyramid of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu</a>, the first attempt at a smooth-sided pyramid. It proved unsuccessful because of miscalculations about the weight placed on the soft ground of sand, gravel and clay, which tended to subside, and its angle changed, which gave it its name. Sneferu then ordered the Red Pyramid, named for its red limestone, a fully built, free-standing, smooth-sided pyramid 104 metres high with an angle of 43 degrees. It is believed to be his resting place.</p><p>Kings of the Middle Kingdom also built at the site. The White Pyramid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-ii">Amenemhat II</a> of the 12th Dynasty was filled with sand and limestone; the weather eroded the sand, and the limestone was taken for other pyramids, so it collapsed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii">Senusret III</a> built a pyramid with galleries and tombs underneath for two princesses, Sit-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a> and Merit. The Black Pyramid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a> is badly eroded, but remains the most imposing monument after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu</a>'s two pyramids, and its polished granite capstone is in the Egyptian Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. Several pyramids of the 13th Dynasty followed, of which only that of Ameny Qemau has been excavated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Undisturbed tombs of royal women found near the pyramid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii">Senusret III</a> held a large amount of lapidary work and jewellery, judged to show the highest stage of metalworking in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in that period. Two treasures of the king's daughters came from a gallery tomb beside the pyramid. Extensive cemeteries of officials of the Old Kingdom and Middle Kingdom have been found around the pyramids. The Bent Pyramid and the Red Pyramid are among the oldest, largest and best preserved pyramids in Egypt.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dahshur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jewellery from the burials of royal women at <b>Dahshur</b> and el-Lahun, such as the pectorals of Sithathoriunet, ranks among the finest Egyptian metalwork.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its monuments defined Egypt for the world: the Step Pyramid of Saqqara, Sneferu's three pyramids at Meidum and <b>Dahshur</b>, the Great Pyramid of Khufu at Giza, with which the fully developed pyramid style was reached, Khafre's second pyramid and, in traditional thinking, the Great Sphinx of Giza, and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-kingdom-of-egypt">Old Kingdom of Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
